{
  "concept": "In an Atwood machine two unequal masses joined by one cord over a frictionless pulley share a single string tension, and the difference between their weights is the net force that gives both blocks equal-magnitude accelerations in opposite directions.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "beige ceiling beam at top left",
    "short gray stem hanging from the beam",
    "gray grooved pulley wheel on the stem",
    "yellow cord draped over the wheel with two vertical ends",
    "wood-grain square block m1 on the left cord end (higher position)",
    "wood-grain square block m2 on the right cord end (lower position)",
    "label 'm1' beside the left block",
    "label 'm2' beside the right block",
    "heading 'Block 1' above the left free-body diagram",
    "heading 'Block 2' above the right free-body diagram",
    "isolated wood-grain square for Block 1 with red upward tension vector labeled T and red downward weight vector labeled w1",
    "isolated wood-grain square for Block 2 with red upward tension vector labeled T and red downward weight vector labeled w2 (longer than w1)",
    "small acceleration arrow pointing up on Block 1 (a1) and down on Block 2 (a2), scaling with the mass difference"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "m2Mass",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Mass m2 (kg), with m1 fixed at 2 kg",
      "range": [1, 6, 0.5],
      "default": 4,
      "teaches": "Changing m2 resizes the two weight arrows and the acceleration arrows, but both tension arrows stay exactly equal in length — one cord means one tension acting on both blocks, while the unequal weights are what produce the acceleration"
    },
    {
      "id": "releaseState",
      "type": "button",
      "label": "Release the system",
      "states": ["held", "released", "later motion"],
      "default": "held",
      "teaches": "Stepping from held to released to later motion shows the heavier block descending while the lighter block rises by exactly the same distance — the connected masses always have equal-magnitude accelerations in opposite directions"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "One cord, one tension",
      "narration": "With the system held and m2 heavier than m1, both free-body diagrams show an identical upward tension T, but the downward weight w2 is longer than w1. The single cord transmits the same tension to both blocks.",
      "state": { "m2Mass": 4, "releaseState": "held" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Sweep the mass difference",
      "narration": "Drag the m2 slider: the weight arrows and the small acceleration arrows grow or shrink, and at m2 = m1 the acceleration arrows vanish, yet the two T arrows remain equal at every setting — the tension is shared, the weight difference drives the motion.",
      "state": { "m2Mass": 6, "releaseState": "held" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Release and watch equal opposite motion",
      "narration": "Step through released to later motion: the heavier block moves down while the lighter block rises by the same distance, making visible that the two connected masses accelerate equally in opposite directions.",
      "state": { "m2Mass": 4, "releaseState": "later motion" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "A single cord over a frictionless pulley forces one shared tension on both masses, so it is the difference of the two weights — not either weight alone — that accelerates the system, and the connected blocks must move with equal-magnitude, opposite-direction accelerations.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Two-zone layout: left third is the pictorial Atwood machine (beige ceiling bar across the top, gray stem and concentric grooved gray wheel, yellow cord dropping to two tan wood-grain squares, m1 higher on the left and m2 lower on the right with italic labels); right two-thirds holds two side-by-side free-body diagrams under 'Block 1' and 'Block 2' headings, each a tan wood-grain square with a red upward arrow labeled T and a red downward arrow labeled w1 / w2. Draw both T arrows the same length; make w2 visibly longer than w1 at the default. Add thin acceleration arrows (up on Block 1, down on Block 2) whose length scales with |m2-m1| and disappears when the masses are equal. On release, translate the two pictorial blocks vertically by equal and opposite amounts while the cord stays taut over the wheel. Use red for all force vectors, keep vector labels in italic serif math style."
}

